<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vikramaditya I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 655–<a href="/years/680">680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vikramaditya I was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> from about 655 to 680, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a>. After the Pallavas had captured Badami in 642 and the kingdom fell into disorder, he expelled them, reunited the Chalukya lands and later raided the Pallava capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. His successors kept the dynasty dominant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> until 753.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vikramaditya was one of several sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a>; the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of Kalyani made him a son of Pulakeshin's son Adityavarman, a statement that can be dismissed as inaccurate. Pulakeshin was defeated and probably killed when the Pallavas invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> around 642, and the history of the next decade is unclear. The throne may have passed to Adityavarman, his son Abhinavaditya and then another brother, Chandraditya, whose widow Vijaya-Bhattarika appears to have acted as regent for their minor son.</p><p>During that regency Vikramaditya seems to have risen as supreme commander of the army and ruler in practice. His inscriptions say that he recovered the regal fortune of his father, which had been concealed by three kings. The historian K. A. Nilakanta Sastri took these to include his brothers and supposed a partition of the kingdom, but there is no evidence of one; D. C. Sircar and others hold that he fought the Pallavas as a subordinate of his brothers and took the throne only after their deaths. His Gadval inscription of about 674, dated to his twelfth regnal year, places his accession about 655.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>With the help of his maternal grandfather, a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a>, Vikramaditya ended a Pallava occupation that had lasted thirteen years. He gave his loyal younger brother Jayasimhavarma the viceroyalty of Lata in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Against the Pallava kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-ii">Mahendravarman II</a> and Paramesvaravarman I he allied himself with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, and by 670 he had a military camp at Malliyur, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. Chalukya records claim that he defeated Paramesvaravarman I and took Kanchi but did not destroy it; the writer Anirudh Kanisetti holds that the city was sacked.</p><p>Chalukya records place him in camp at Uragapura on the south bank of the Kaveri river on 25 April 674. At Peruvalanallur he met Paramesvaravarman I and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king, and Pallava and Pandya records alike claim that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> were defeated there. Pallava inscriptions also speak of the destruction of the city of Ranarasika, an epithet of Vikramaditya, but several historians question whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> was sacked a second time; Chalukya records credit his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapida">Vinayaditya</a> and grandson Vijayaditya with defeating the invaders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vikramaditya bore titles that advertised his military power, among them Rana-rasika, lover of war, Anivarita, unopposed, and Raja-malla, royal wrestler. He was married to the Western Ganga princess Gangamahadevi. Under him the Chalukya kingdom recovered most of the territory it had controlled under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a>. He died in 680 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapida">Vinayaditya</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vikramaditya I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vikramaditya I</b> expelled the Pallavas by about 655 and raided Kanchipuram in return, and Vikramaditya II occupied Kanchipuram around 740.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukyas recovered under <b>Vikramaditya I</b>, and around 740 Vikramaditya II occupied Kanchipuram, though he spared its temples and left a Kannada inscription in the Kailasanatha temple recording his gifts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
